WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.540
After a chance encounter with a young David
Letterman, Chris Elliott went from being a

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.800
mild-mannered NBC gofer to a writer and performer
on Letterman's groundbreaking talk show.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:15.480
From 1982 to 1989, Elliott honed his craft,
helping the program to earn multiple Emmys

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.039
for Outstanding Writing in a Variety Series.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:22.449
Those early years helped to pave the way for
the quirky actor, best known for his scene-stealing

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:27.040
roles in films such as Groundhog Day, There's
Something About Mary, and Scary Movie.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.120
"I'm Hanson, I'm the caretaker."

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.280
But since the early 2000s, Elliott has kept
a fairly low profile.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.600
Here's what he's been up to.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.940
Weird from the start

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.760
First, a bit of backstory: Chris Elliott's
father, Bob Elliott, was a popular comedian

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:48.539
and radio personality in the 1950s, most notable
for his legendary work with comedy partner

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.380
Ray Goulding.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.499
David Letterman's love for Bob's work helped
Chris get his career-changing opportunity

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.260
at Late Night.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.000
In 1990, Elliott teamed with two guys who
used to work on Late Night cast members to

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.680
bring the short-lived sitcom Get a Life to
Fox.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.100
Featuring writers like Mr. Show's Bob Odenkirk
and Adaptation's Charlie Kaufman, it's safe

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.820
to say the program was different from anything
on TV.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.200
"Don't you see, Sharon?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.940
He's teaching us about love!"

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.751
It was all too weird for Fox, and the network
pulled the plug after just two seasons, but

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.500
it's considered a cult classic today.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.560
Two years later, Elliott co-wrote and starred
in another genuinely strange piece of entertainment:

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.880
the Tim Burton-produced flop Cabin Boy, which
also enjoys cult status today.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.500
Elliott starred as snobby aristocrat-turned
sea captain Nathaniel Mayweather in what is

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.960
certainly the weirdest movie of his career,
which also featured a cameo from his old pal

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.160
David Letterman ...

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.600
"I realize that you are most likely the product
of lower-class inbreeding, but perhaps you

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.160
could help me."

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.020
"Oh gosh, I certainly hope so."

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.420
Steady guest star

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.740
His first film as a leading man was critical
and financial disaster, but Elliott soon found

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.320
his footing.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.500
After a string of small roles in high-profile
flicks in the late '90s and early-2000s, Elliott

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.609
has continued working steadily, primarily
in supporting and guest-starring stints on

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:08.220
a number of popular TV sitcoms and dramas.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.310
Alongside small roles in more than a dozen
shows like Law &amp; Order: SVU, Difficult People,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.690
and The Good Wife, he appeared in ten episodes
of Everybody Loves Raymond as Amy's comic

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.360
book-loving older brother Peter.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.780
He was also played Lily's father, Mickey,
in multiple seasons of How I Met Your Mother.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.940
In October 2017, Elliott made his guest-starring
debut on Fox's apocalypse-themed comedy The

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.600
Last Man on Earth, playing a castaway named
Glenn.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.720
Failed family sitcom

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:39.080
In 2006, Elliott had plans to bring his family
to television with the sitcom You've Reached

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:39.700
the Elliotts.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.080
The CBS series was going to follow Elliott,
a husband and father of two struggling with

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.820
the balance of raising a family in Connecticut
as he pursued a B-list acting career in Hollywood.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.340
Splitsider described the concept as Get a
Life crossed with Everybody Loves Raymond,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.840
except the series would've taken a meta look
at Elliott's real life through a cringe-inducing

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:58.840
comedic lens.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.400
While this sounds like a winning formula,
with meta-comedy hits like Curb Your Enthusiasm

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.840
changing the small-screen landscape, the project
was ultimately passed on by the network.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:09.900
Late Night reunion

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:15.020
Elliott returned sporadically to Late Night
with David Letterman during the 2007/2008

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.880
season, more than two decades since his Emmy-winning
work on the CBS staple.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.020
He appeared in multiple sketches throughout
the year as recurring characters, including

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.260
Skink, a bounty hunter with some unorthodox
methods ...

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.840
"What we do is, we basically cheese the guy
until he's down there in that hot box, y'know,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.700
wearing the ol' flop suit."

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:34.440
"Wow."

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.740
Back to his roots

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:42.250
When Adult Swim's Eagleheart came calling
in 2011, things came full circle for Elliott,

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.060
with a starring role on a TV show suited for
his oddball sensibilities.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:50.560
Eagleheart's creator, Jason Woliner, was a
big fan of Elliott's work in Get a Life and

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.900
Cabin Boy, and while the show wasn't written
with him in mind, it was reworked to suit

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.020
his comedic stylings.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:01.060
In 2015, Elliott joined the cast of a Canadian
sitcom with a ridiculous name we can't say

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:01.560
here.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.420
The Christopher Guest-style comedy reunites
Catherine O'Hara with Eugene Levy as formerly

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.040
wealthy magnates forced to move to a small
town.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.860
The hilarity is capped off by Chris Elliott's
performance as Roland, the white trash, mullet-wearing,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.240
beer-guzzling mayor of the Roses' new home.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.900
Since hitting Netflix, it's found a new audience,
and was renewed for a fourth season in March

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:21.500
2017.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.620
Comedy legacy

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.420
Elliott passed the comedy torch to his two
daughters Abby and Bridey.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.080
Bridey has worked a lot in the world of stand-up
comedy, and has also acted in the TBS series

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.050
Search Party, HBO's Silicon Valley, and the
Emma Stone/Steve Carell movie Battle of the

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:37.540
Sexes.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.980
Like her father before her, Abby appeared
as a cast member on Saturday Night Live from

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:48.180
2008 to 2012, making her the first ever second-generation
performer on the long-running sketch series.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:49.680
Thanks for watching!

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:52.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:55.080
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

